    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
      Relocating to a new country is an exhilarating career milestone, yet it’s often accompanied by an immense administrative burden. Singapore is globally renowned as a premier destination for global executives, tech talent, and expatriates holding <strong>Employment Passes (EP), Overseas Networks & Expertise Passes (ONE Pass), S Passes, or Work Permits</strong>. The city-state boasts world-class infrastructure, exceptional safety, and an incredibly robust economy. However, underneath the surface of this vibrant metropolis lies a complex tax framework. Calculating your net take-home pay requires far more than just dividing your annual salary by twelve. It requires a clear understanding of the quantitative 183-day tax residency rule, non-resident flat tax benchmarks, the tax treatment of housing allowances and stock options (ESOPs), and the mandatory, sometimes anxiety-inducing, tax clearance via <strong>Form IR21</strong> when it's time to move on. 
    </p>

