    <h2 id="residency-rules" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. EP Tax Residency & 183-Day Rule: The Foundation of Your Tax Bill</h2>
    <p>
      The single most crucial factor determining your tax liability in Singapore is your tax residency status. Under <a href="https://www.iras.gov.sg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline">IRAS statutory rules</a>, an expatriate holding an Employment Pass, S Pass, or ONE Pass is classified as a Singapore tax resident if they physically stay or work in Singapore for <strong>183 days or more in a calendar year</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This rule often catches new arrivals off guard. Let’s say you relocate to Singapore to start a new job on August 1st. By the end of December, you will have only been in the country for 153 days. Because this is less than 183 days in that specific calendar year, you are technically a <strong>non-resident</strong> for tax purposes for that first year, which subjects you to a different, often higher, tax regime. The stress of counting days and planning vacations around this threshold is a common anxiety for many expats during their first year.
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface my-6">
      <h3 class="font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary text-lg mb-2">Multi-Year Administrative Rules: The Expat's Lifeline</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-3">To alleviate the burden on foreigners who arrive late in the year, IRAS provides generous administrative concessions. These are crucial safety nets you must know about:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-sm space-y-2 text-muted">
        <li><strong>2-Year Administrative Rule:</strong> If you work in Singapore for 2 continuous calendar years, you are treated as a tax resident for both years, provided your total stay spans at least 183 days across the two years combined. This means arriving in August won't permanently penalize you if you stay through the next year.</li>
        <li><strong>3-Year Administrative Rule:</strong> If your employment spans 3 continuous calendar years (e.g., arriving in November 2024, staying all of 2025, and leaving in February 2026), you are treated as a tax resident for all 3 years regardless of the exact day count in the first and third year. This provides immense peace of mind for standard 2-to-3 year expat assignments.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1560518883-ce09059eeffa?w=1200&q=80" alt="Singapore Marina Bay Financial Centre skyscraper towers" class="my-8 w-full rounded-xl object-cover max-h-96 shadow-md" loading="lazy" />

    <h2 id="resident-vs-nonresident" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. Tax Rate Benchmark: Progressive vs 15% Flat Rate</h2>
    <p>
      Understanding how your residency status impacts your wallet is where the rubber meets the road. The tax calculation for foreign professionals varies dramatically based on whether IRAS views you as a resident or a non-resident.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a <strong>Tax Resident</strong>, you enjoy Singapore's famous progressive tax rates. These rates range from 0% on the first S$20,000 of income, up to 24% for income exceeding S$1,000,000. Most importantly, residents are eligible for personal tax reliefs—deductions that lower your taxable income, such as the Earned Income Relief, Spouse Relief, and Child Relief.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a <strong>Non-Resident</strong> (someone who works in Singapore for 61 to 182 days in a calendar year without qualifying for multi-year concessions), the rules are starkly different. You will be taxed at a flat rate of 15% on your employment income, OR the resident progressive rate without any personal reliefs—whichever results in a higher tax amount. This can lead to surprisingly large tax bills for short-term assignees who aren't prepared.
    </p>

